module Darklang.Cli.Docs.ForAI

let content(): String =
  """# Darklang AI Quick Reference

"""
  + SyntaxAtAGlance.content()
  + """
Saved code lives in a persistent package tree, not in files. Use your own namespace;
`Darklang` is reserved.

## Build, run, test

  module /MyApp.Greet - <<'EOF'
  let greeting(name: String): String = "Hello, " + name
  let main(args: List<String>): Int64 =
    match args with
    | [ name ] ->
      Stdlib.printLine(greeting(name))
      0
    | _ -> 2
  module Tests =
    test hello = MyApp.Greet.greeting("Ada") |> Stdlib.Test.equal("Hello, Ada")
  EOF
  run MyApp.Greet.main Ada               # args = ["Ada"]; stdin is piped in as usual
  test run MyApp.Greet
  edit MyApp.Greet.greeting --patch - <<'PATCH'   # lines exactly as `view <name> --raw` shows
  @@
   let greeting(name: String): String =
  -  "Hello, " + name
  +  "Hi, " + name
  PATCH
  rename MyApp.Greet.greeting MyApp.Greet.salutation   # updates callers
  search --batch --exact --fn --brief Stdlib.List.map Stdlib.Json.parse
  search --fn --limit 3 "get a value from a dictionary"
  docs signatures Stdlib.String          # every function in a module

For a one-off, write a script: plain statements, no module or `main`. It saves
nothing, gets its arguments as `args`, and its last value is the exit code (Unit is
0); see `docs scripts`.

  run - Ada <<'EOF'                      # `-`: script on stdin
  Stdlib.printLine("Hello, " + Stdlib.String.join(args, " "))
  EOF

Use a module for code you will rerun, test, edit or serve.

Fails or answers wrong? `traces record on`, run it again, then `traces show <fn>`
(a failure prints the exact line): every value, no prints needed.

## Permissions

Printing, reading stdin, the clock and random numbers need no permission. Anything else
needs `--allow <rule>` on `run`, before the function name:

  run --allow "file read $PWD" --allow "file write $PWD" MyApp.Report.main sales.csv
                                         # $PWD: this folder and below
  run --allow 'http GET https://api.example.com/*' MyApp.Fetch.main
                                         # any path on that host; `*` only after a `/`

`serve` takes no `--allow`; allow what it needs first: `permissions allow http-server
127.0.0.1 <port>`, and `permissions allow file read <dir>` / `file write <dir>`.

Effects are inferred, never declared; a save lists them (`effects: none`). Keep pure
code pure: pass in what reads, writes or fetches as an argument instead of calling it.

## Functions most programs need

Anything that can fail returns a Result or an Option: unwrap with `match`,
`Stdlib.Result.withDefault(r, x)`, or `r?` in a function that returns a Result.

Can fail:
  Stdlib.Int64.parse(s)   Stdlib.Float64.parse(s)                // Result
  Stdlib.String.slice(s, from, to)                               // Result
  Stdlib.Utf8.decode(bytes)   Stdlib.Json.parse<Profile>(text)   // Result
  Stdlib.Cli.FileSystem.readFileAsString(path)                   // Result<String, Posix.Error>
  Stdlib.Cli.FileSystem.writeFile(path, Stdlib.Utf8.encode(s))   // Result
  Stdlib.HttpClient.get(url, [("user-agent", "me")])             // Result; then r.statusCode, r.body
  Stdlib.List.getAt(xs, i)   Stdlib.List.randomElement(xs)       // Option
  Stdlib.Dict.get(d, k)                                          // Option; Stdlib.Dict.set(d, k, v) adds
  Stdlib.Cli.Stdin.readLineOrEnd()                               // Option; None at end of input

Always give a value:
  Stdlib.String.split(s, ",")   Stdlib.String.join(xs, ",")   Stdlib.String.trim(s)
  Stdlib.List.contains(xs, x)   Stdlib.String.fromChar(c)   Stdlib.Json.serialize<Profile>(v)
  Stdlib.Int64.toFloat64(n)   Stdlib.Float64.round(x)            // round gives an Int64
  Stdlib.List.parallelMap(xs, f)                                 // runs f concurrently
  Stdlib.Int64.random(1, 6)                                      // both ends included
  Stdlib.DateTime.now()   Stdlib.DateTime.subtractMs(later, earlier)    // gap in ms, an Int64
  Stdlib.Cli.Posix.sleep(1000.0)                                 // ms, as a Float64
  Stdlib.Decimal.round(19.999dec, 2)      // Ok(20.00dec). To round, use Decimal
  Stdlib.List.indexedMap(xs, fun(i, x) -> $"{i}: {x}")   Stdlib.max(a, b)   Stdlib.abs(n)

Easy to miss:
  Stdlib.Cli.Process.run("git", ["status"])      // Result; out.exitCode, out.stdout, no shell
  Stdlib.Csv.parse(text)   Stdlib.Crypto.sha256(bytes)   Stdlib.Html.escapeHtml(s)
  Stdlib.Cli.Dir.createRecursive(path)   Stdlib.Cli.FileSystem.listDirectory(dir)  // full paths
  Stdlib.DateTime.format(d, "%Y-%m-%d")          // % directives, not yyyy-MM-dd

## Common slips

- `$"{x}"` shows any value; there is no `Int64.toString`. Inside `{…}` never write `\"…\"`.
- Commas between record fields, never `;`. A lambda takes no type annotations.
- A Dict type names both key and value: `Dict<String, Float64>`; an empty one is `Dict {}`.
- `{ s with count = 1 }` updates a record. `Some((a, b))` keeps the tuple's parentheses.
- `|>` passes the first argument; an `if` or `match` on its left needs parentheses.
- An HTTP request runs in the background until used. To time one, `Stdlib.await(...)` it
  before reading the clock again.

Output read by a program (shell, pipe) is plain: one record per line, tab-separated fields.

Logs in ~/.darklang/logs/: cli.log (errors that ended a command), host-audit.jsonl
(every file, network, env and process access), telemetry.jsonl (DARK_TELEMETRY=1).

## More
  docs for-ai packages | tests | permissions | traces | scm | syntax
  docs for-ai tests http-server   several parts and topics in one call
  help <command>"""
